Python's socket module gives you TCP for free. The point of this course project
was to not use it, and instead build the reliability, ordering, and congestion
behavior TCP normally provides, using only plain UDP datagrams underneath.

The result is a socket-like class with the same core methods as a real socket,
`bind`, `listen`, `accept`, `connect`, `send`, `receive`, `close`, so code
written against it looks like ordinary networking code, even though everything
underneath is built from scratch.

## What it does

Connections go through a real three-way handshake and a proper TCP-style
teardown, with separate states for each stage of closing rather than just
dropping packets. Every segment carries a sequence number, the receiver sends
cumulative acknowledgements, and out-of-order segments are buffered and
stitched back into place once the missing pieces arrive.

Loss is handled two ways: a segment is retransmitted if it times out, and also
immediately if three duplicate acknowledgements arrive, the standard signal
that a segment was lost rather than just delayed. A congestion window grows
through slow start and congestion avoidance and shrinks on loss, and a
separate receiver window enforces flow control, including probing the
receiver when its window drops to zero. The retransmission timeout itself is
not fixed. It adapts from measured round-trip times using the standard
estimator TCP itself uses.

Packets can also carry an encrypted payload, using AES in counter mode, as an
optional flag on each packet rather than a separate protocol.

## Demo

A small multi-client chat application, a server and a client, runs on top of
the socket to exercise it under real, unpredictable timing rather than in a
controlled test.

